# Orthonormal Basis for the Tangent Space

## Setup

Let $M$ be a smooth $n$-dimensional Riemannian manifold with Riemannian metric $g$. Fix a point $p \in M$. The **tangent space** $T_p M$ is the $n$-dimensional real vector space of tangent vectors at $p$. The metric $g$ restricts to a positive-definite inner product on $T_p M$, which we denote by
$$\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle_g : T_p M \times T_p M \to \mathbb{R}, \qquad \langle X, Y \rangle_g = g_p(X, Y).$$

## Definition

A collection of tangent vectors $\{e_1, e_2, \dots, e_n\} \subset T_p M$ is an **orthonormal basis** of the tangent space $T_p M$ (with respect to the Riemannian metric $g$) if:

1. **Basis property**: $\{e_1, \dots, e_n\}$ is a basis of $T_p M$ as a real vector space, i.e., the vectors are linearly independent and $\dim T_p M = n$ of them span the space.

2. **Orthonormality condition**: The vectors are pairwise orthogonal and each has unit length, expressed compactly as
$$\boxed{\;\langle e_i, e_j \rangle_g = \delta_{ij} \quad \text{for all } 1 \le i, j \le n\;}$$
where $\delta_{ij}$ is the Kronecker delta:
$$\delta_{ij} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } i = j, \\ 0 & \text{if } i \neq j. \end{cases}$$

Equivalently, writing the condition out:
- **Unit length**: $\|e_i\|_g = \sqrt{\langle e_i, e_i \rangle_g} = 1$ for every $i$.
- **Mutual orthogonality**: $\langle e_i, e_j \rangle_g = 0$ whenever $i \neq j$.

## Existence (why the definition is non-vacuous)

Since $g_p$ is a positive-definite inner product on the finite-dimensional vector space $T_p M$, the Gram–Schmidt process applies. Starting from *any* basis $\{v_1, \dots, v_n\}$ of $T_p M$, define recursively
$$e_1 = \frac{v_1}{\|v_1\|_g}, \qquad e_k = \frac{v_k - \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} \langle v_k, e_i \rangle_g \, e_i}{\left\| v_k - \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} \langle v_k, e_i \rangle_g \, e_i \right\|_g} \quad (k \ge 2).$$
A straightforward induction shows $\langle e_i, e_j \rangle_g = \delta_{ij}$, so an orthonormal basis always exists. Uniqueness does **not** hold: any orthogonal transformation of an orthonormal basis yields another, so the set of orthonormal bases is in bijection with the orthogonal group $O(n)$.

## Coordinate expression

If $(U, x = (x^1, \dots, x^n))$ is a local chart around $p$ with coordinate basis $\{\partial/\partial x^1, \dots, \partial/\partial x^n\}$, and we write $e_i = e_i^{\,a}\, \partial/\partial x^a$ (summation over $a$), then the orthonormality condition becomes
$$g_{ab}(p)\, e_i^{\,a}\, e_j^{\,b} = \delta_{ij},$$
where $g_{ab}(p) = g_p(\partial/\partial x^a, \partial/\partial x^b)$ are the components of the metric tensor at $p$. In matrix form, if $E = (e_i^{\,a})$ is the $n \times n$ matrix whose $i$-th column is $e_i$, and $G = (g_{ab})$ is the metric matrix, the condition reads
$$E^{\mathsf{T}} G\, E = I_n.$$

## Summary

An orthonormal basis of $T_p M$ is a basis $\{e_1, \dots, e_n\}$ satisfying $\langle e_i, e_j \rangle_g = \delta_{ij}$ for all $i, j$ — unit length and mutual orthogonality under the Riemannian inner product. Such a basis always exists by Gram–Schmidt and is unique only up to the action of $O(n)$.

